{
  "type": "say",
  "text": "Thanks, Theo. Both findings are significant. The billing service constraint — that it can only emit reliably through a transactional outbox into Kafka — effectively eliminates direct Kafka publishing and the managed cloud queue as options for that service, which means the transactional-outbox-to-Kafka design is the only architecture that can accommodate all services. And the 12-partition requirement for peak throughput is noted as a hard configuration parameter. Before I record the final design, let me make sure I'm synthesizing everything correctly. Mina, from compliance: 7-year immutable retention with customer-managed encryption, causal ordering per service, cryptographic tamper-evidence via chaining or hashing, access logging on the audit data itself, and queryable indexing for external auditor retrieval. Theo, from data platform: billing service requires transactional-outbox-to-Kafka, and exactly 12 Kafka partitions for peak throughput. Given that the billing service constraint forces the transactional-outbox-to-Kafka approach, and that approach also satisfies the ordering and reliability requirements from compliance, I'm prepared to settle on the transactional-outbox-to-Kafka design as our production architecture. Are there any objections or anything else either of you need on the record before I record the final design and follow-up commitments?"
}
